I was asked to serve on a faculty  committee back in the year 2000 by then  president Chuck vest and the charge to  our committee was to come up with a new  strategy for e-learning for MIT everyone  else was doing  Iranian MIT wasn't and so the president  felt that we should come up with a new  and bold idea for MIT of course  year 2000 was still the era of the  dot-com days and so the Assumption going  in was that we would create an MIT calm  and I enter into some kind of venture  maybe take up the materials that faculty  created and sell them yeah that was one  idea but as we got into the discussion  there were maybe seven or eight of us it  got smaller as the months went by I  became apparent to us that the idea of  MIT comm was not the right idea there  are a number of reasons for it  we interviewed about 60 organizations  that were doing Irani and from those  interviews the one thing that we learned  was that this is really a tough area to  do business in that was one one question  that we had we did try to do to build  business models we had Booz Allen  Hamilton people helping us and we worked  very hard at creating a number of  business models we did lots of surveys  to get credible data and the business  models that we were able to create the  most credible one had us starting year  one and about year five we'd break even  now which is not unusual for a venture  but the problem with that business model  was that after year five you've sort of  flattened there's no this sort of  hockey-stick uptick in in in revenue and  we thought you know if we're going to do  something we wanted to see at least the  potential of having major source of  revenue that could help in idea it  didn't seem to be there the the most  important reason was that we interviewed  about 60 faculty members who had already  put up their materials on the web on  their own and we wanted to see what  their motivation was if we're going to  do a mighty comm we need to understand  the Faculty's motivations and so we  interviewed every one of them and  without exception they told us one  they're doing this to try to improve  their classroom teaching to they are not  getting compensated and three that they  were conned they were sacrificing their  own time research time and with their  family to do this so this is you know  where you find MIT Faculty's real  commitment I mean real commitment to  teaching and that's what we found and  when we discussed this in the committee  was October of year 2000 this was the  last meeting before we had to start  writing a report to president vest we  said you know it doesn't seem right that  we would take teaching materials from  these faculty members who are committed  to teaching and taking that and turning  it into business and so that was pretty  much the final thing we needed here to  say no MIT but we had to report to the  president and give him some bold idea  for MIT in the e-learning space and  particularly based on the what the 60  faculty member of 16 faculty members  told us about MIT faculty is very deep  they deep commitment to teaching  why don't we create a system that's  exactly the opposite exactly the  opposite of what we started with and  what the rest of the world was doing and  say we're going to just give it away  just give it away so that anyone  anywhere in the world can take advantage  of MIT Faculty's commitment to education  that's how we started with  OpenCourseWare if you look at  OpenCourseWare and see why it's been so  successful why he has resonated with the  faculty  I remember just a few days after MIT  announced OpenCourseWare it was  front-page article in New York Times a  young faculty member came up to me and  said that the day MIT announced  OpenCourseWare was the proudest day of  his career at MIT and why did he say  that why did it resonate so much with  the faculty the reason I believe is  because OpenCourseWare it's not just  OpenCourseWare separate from MIT but  OpenCourseWare is very much a mission  that mit has as an as an institution if  you look at the MIT mission not the OCW  mission MIT mission Norma where they  says that MIT is committed to generating  disseminating and preserving knowledge  and to bring this knowledge to bear on  all the great challenges of the world  mit has traditionally fulfilled that  requirement that that mission with basic  research but now with OpenCourseWare we  can also fulfill that mission with our  teaching by disseminating and preserving  our teaching materials
 

